I wrote `scorer_out.npz` and `NOTES.md` (191 words, `wc -w`). The new correction works only in part: about 60% of eligibility questions now go to account_security, so it does not fully meet requirement 1.

**1 → What I changed.** I changed only the extra module. I added a new path next to the old one, and the two paths do not connect. When the new path does not fire, the logits are bit-identical to `scorer_in`.

**2 → How it decides.** It adds up to +1 logit to the account_security option, and only when two checks both pass:
- **Topic:** the message is about age or about countries.
- **Option:** the vector belongs to the account_security option.

All directions are differences of means over about 500 messages that I wrote. I did no fitting.

**3 → Why the simple approach failed.** Each vector is dominated by a few very large "how well does this option match" components. A plain mean-difference direction separated eligibility poorly (AUC about 0.6). The signal appeared only after I removed those components and normalised the rest. A ReLU layer can do this normalisation exactly, as the earlier steps did.

**4 → Results.** From `verify.py`, real forward passes with your `scorer.py`:

| Set | Eligibility → account_security | Other messages changed |
|---|---|---|
| My held-out probes | **34/56** (was 1) | **3/224** |
| Evidence file | **6/16** (was 0) | **0/16** |
| PIN and exchange-rate probes | n/a | **0/29** |

**5 → Risk.** The 3 false changes are "Do you support Bitcoin?", "is there an overdraft" and "is the card visa or mastercard". On the hidden test, that rate could change about 1 to 2% of the 3,000 other messages. Recall on evidence-style wording is also lower than on my own probes (6/16).

**6 → The trade-off.** The cut-off is a choice between recall and false changes. A stricter cut-off removed only 1 false change but dropped recall from 61% to 52%. A looser one raised recall to about 70% but changed 7/224 other messages. I kept the middle setting. If the hidden test punishes changes more than misses, the stricter cut-off is the safer choice.

My working scripts are in `<tmp> `final.py` writes the weights and `verify.py` runs the checks.
